WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.548 --> 00:00:14.968
Four years ago,

00:00:14.992 --> 00:00:17.984
a teenage girl changed my life

00:00:18.008 --> 00:00:20.238
in one conversation.

00:00:20.262 --> 00:00:21.579
She was 13 years old,

00:00:21.603 --> 00:00:23.260
she was a friend's little cousin

00:00:23.284 --> 00:00:24.482
and she casually told me

00:00:24.506 --> 00:00:27.773
that she had met the man
she was going to marry.

00:00:28.577 --> 00:00:31.490
So I said, "OK, tell me about him."

00:00:31.894 --> 00:00:35.021
And she told me that his name
was Harry Styles.

00:00:35.045 --> 00:00:36.664
(Laughter)

00:00:36.688 --> 00:00:38.664
So I laughed a little, like you,

00:00:38.688 --> 00:00:41.283
and then she said,
"I know you don't think I'm serious,

00:00:41.307 --> 00:00:43.221
but I'm actually going to be with him.

00:00:43.245 --> 00:00:44.900
Because I love him so much

00:00:44.924 --> 00:00:47.352
that I would slit someone's
throat to be with him."

00:00:47.376 --> 00:00:50.853
(Laughter)

00:00:50.877 --> 00:00:52.615
And that was the moment

00:00:52.639 --> 00:00:57.060
that I became obsessed with fangirls.

00:00:57.084 --> 00:00:58.318
I didn't know it then,

00:00:58.342 --> 00:01:02.116
but that moment would transform
the course of my life

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.370
and go on to change everything
that I thought I knew

00:01:05.394 --> 00:01:06.926
about being an adult,

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.125
being a woman

00:01:08.149 --> 00:01:09.926
and being truly happy.

00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:11.926
But before we get started,

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:13.228
what is a fangirl,

00:01:13.252 --> 00:01:15.403
and what is a Harry Styles?

00:01:15.427 --> 00:01:18.871
Well, according to the dictionary,
the Merriam-Webster dictionary,

00:01:18.895 --> 00:01:21.664
a fangirl is a "girl or woman

00:01:21.688 --> 00:01:24.950
who is an extremely
or overly enthusiastic fan

00:01:24.974 --> 00:01:27.219
of someone or something."

00:01:27.243 --> 00:01:29.482
Technically, you can have
fangirls of anything,

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:33.125
but my specific interest
was in fangirls of boy bands.

00:01:33.149 --> 00:01:36.117
Because of their somewhat
lethal reputation.

00:01:36.554 --> 00:01:40.752
I remember, my dad had told me this story
of some Beatles fans in the '60s,

00:01:40.776 --> 00:01:45.141
who apparently had torn
a parked BMW to literal pieces,

00:01:45.165 --> 00:01:47.662
because the band had supposedly
just been sat in it.

00:01:48.416 --> 00:01:52.408
In the '60s, the Beatles
were the biggest boy band on the planet,

00:01:52.432 --> 00:01:55.265
but when I met this girl in 2015,

00:01:55.289 --> 00:02:00.090
the biggest boy band on the planet
was none other than One Direction.

00:02:00.114 --> 00:02:04.188
And Harry Styles was a member
of One Direction.

00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:07.958
Harry Styles was reputed
for his compassionate demeanor

00:02:07.982 --> 00:02:09.505
and perfect hair.

00:02:10.188 --> 00:02:13.823
I learn this when I read thousands
of tweets about him.

00:02:13.847 --> 00:02:15.847
I learn that he is a sweet cupcake.

00:02:15.871 --> 00:02:17.919
I learn that he is a perfect angel.

00:02:17.943 --> 00:02:19.109
I learn that one time,

00:02:19.133 --> 00:02:21.760
he vomited on the side
of a freeway in California

00:02:21.784 --> 00:02:23.395
and that within two hours,

00:02:23.419 --> 00:02:26.331
fans had turned the site of the vomit
into a sacred shrine.

00:02:26.355 --> 00:02:27.664
(Laughter)

00:02:27.688 --> 00:02:28.956
I scroll through --

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.535
(Laughter)

00:02:31.559 --> 00:02:34.527
I scroll through fan-made
paintings of Harry,

00:02:34.551 --> 00:02:35.963
baby photos of him,

00:02:35.987 --> 00:02:38.320
paintings of baby photos of him.

00:02:38.344 --> 00:02:42.127
I watch videos that show me how to make
DIY love totems for Harry --

00:02:42.151 --> 00:02:45.522
for example, a lampshade
covered in photos of his face,

00:02:45.546 --> 00:02:48.808
or a key ring that states
the exact time of his birth.

00:02:49.188 --> 00:02:50.887
I read hours of fan fiction,

00:02:50.911 --> 00:02:53.299
and I fall down this specific rabbit hole

00:02:53.323 --> 00:02:56.545
of stories that actually
place me as a protagonist

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:59.403
inside of various imagined
romances with him.

00:02:59.427 --> 00:03:02.807
So in one, I tell him
that I'm pregnant with his child.

00:03:03.315 --> 00:03:06.528
In another, we meet in hospital
where we're both fighting cancer,

00:03:06.552 --> 00:03:09.329
and in another, we fall so deeply in love

00:03:09.353 --> 00:03:11.697
that we become fugitives who kill people.

00:03:11.721 --> 00:03:13.123
(Laughter)

00:03:13.679 --> 00:03:14.829
But then ...

00:03:15.784 --> 00:03:17.784
something unthinkable happens.

00:03:19.252 --> 00:03:23.196
One Direction, the biggest
boy band on the planet,

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:24.370
loses a member.

00:03:25.514 --> 00:03:28.474
Zayn Malik quits the band,

00:03:28.498 --> 00:03:32.149
and the internet explodes with feels.

00:03:32.797 --> 00:03:36.948
I read tweets as these girls describe
the physical pain of this loss,

00:03:36.972 --> 00:03:40.225
how they can't eat or sleep or walk.

00:03:40.249 --> 00:03:43.714
I read them describe
how much Zayn had meant to them.

00:03:43.738 --> 00:03:46.690
And I watch videos
of 10-year-old girls crying.

00:03:48.079 --> 00:03:49.679
But, like, really crying.

00:03:50.968 --> 00:03:54.284
And then I watch as people
repost these videos but with new titles

00:03:54.308 --> 00:03:58.168
that contain words like "crazy"
and "creepy" and "insane."

00:03:58.192 --> 00:04:00.898
And suddenly, my YouTube sidebar contains

00:04:00.922 --> 00:04:04.422
"Compilation: Fans react to Zayn leaving.

00:04:04.446 --> 00:04:05.985
Psycho alert!"

00:04:06.359 --> 00:04:09.990
Then I watch as mainstream
news outlets cover the story.

00:04:10.014 --> 00:04:12.823
I read them describe
these "young banshees."

00:04:12.847 --> 00:04:14.632
I read one journalist say,

00:04:14.656 --> 00:04:17.641
"It's a commonly known fact
since the age of the Beatles

00:04:17.665 --> 00:04:19.814
that there is nothing
scarier in this world

00:04:19.838 --> 00:04:22.206
than a group of excited teenage girls."

00:04:22.230 --> 00:04:23.230
(Laughter)

00:04:24.205 --> 00:04:28.244
And then I ask myself a question
I've never considered in my life.

00:04:28.967 --> 00:04:34.957
Why is it that the image of young girls
screaming their lungs out with excitement

00:04:34.981 --> 00:04:36.442
for a pop star

00:04:36.466 --> 00:04:40.315
is considered crazy, psycho,

00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:42.934
scary, a bit much?

00:04:42.958 --> 00:04:48.529
But the image of young boys
screaming their lungs out for a footballer

00:04:48.553 --> 00:04:49.887
is perfectly normal?

00:04:50.919 --> 00:04:52.653
Boys crying at the footie,

00:04:52.677 --> 00:04:54.544
that's the love of the game.

00:04:54.903 --> 00:04:57.569
Girls crying at a Justin Bieber concert?

00:04:57.981 --> 00:04:59.131
That's pathetic.

00:04:59.895 --> 00:05:02.315
And as soon as I realized
this double standard,

00:05:02.339 --> 00:05:05.022
I realized that all
of my curiosity about fangirls

00:05:05.046 --> 00:05:07.776
had been sparked
by exactly the same judgments.

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:11.098
I, too, had suspected
that they were a bit crazy.

00:05:11.624 --> 00:05:14.523
I'd looked at images
of girls screaming for the Beatles,

00:05:14.547 --> 00:05:16.300
the Backstreet Boys, One Direction,

00:05:16.324 --> 00:05:19.222
and the word that had come to mind
was not "excitement"

00:05:19.246 --> 00:05:20.530
but "hysteria."

00:05:21.102 --> 00:05:23.770
And what I did not know
was the history of that word.

00:05:24.086 --> 00:05:25.999
That in the 19th century,

00:05:26.023 --> 00:05:30.381
hysteria was considered to be
a legitimate female mental disorder

00:05:30.405 --> 00:05:32.537
that could be diagnosed by doctors

00:05:32.561 --> 00:05:37.101
if women displayed excessive emotion
or difficult behavior.

00:05:37.847 --> 00:05:40.839
The word "hysterical" comes
from the Latin word "hystericus,"

00:05:40.863 --> 00:05:42.514
meaning "of the womb,"

00:05:42.538 --> 00:05:46.655
because it was thought that this condition
was caused by a dysfunction of the uterus.

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:50.339
And so, a treatment for hysteria

00:05:50.363 --> 00:05:51.630
was a hysterectomy.

00:05:52.490 --> 00:05:55.233
Which is what we still call
a removal of the womb.

00:05:55.781 --> 00:05:57.027
And at this point,

00:05:57.051 --> 00:05:59.995
I decide to redeclare my obsession.

00:06:00.019 --> 00:06:03.432
Because I am no longer
just obsessed with fangirls.

00:06:03.456 --> 00:06:08.494
Now, I'm obsessed with the way
that the world talks about fangirls

00:06:08.518 --> 00:06:14.327
and the way that the world looks
at young, female enthusiasm.

00:06:15.510 --> 00:06:18.245
Because, I want to know,

00:06:18.269 --> 00:06:21.094
if girls grow up in a world

00:06:21.118 --> 00:06:26.142
where words like "crazy" and "psycho"
and "hysterical" are casually used

00:06:26.166 --> 00:06:29.085
to describe female enthusiasm,

00:06:29.109 --> 00:06:33.212
then how does that shape the way
that those girls get to see themselves?

00:06:33.601 --> 00:06:37.902
And if girls grow up
in a world that tells them

00:06:37.926 --> 00:06:42.387
that they are designed
just a bit crazier than the boys,

00:06:42.411 --> 00:06:44.706
then isn't that a little bit
like telling them

00:06:44.730 --> 00:06:48.450
that they are born less capable
of rationality than men,

00:06:49.077 --> 00:06:50.611
less capable of reason

00:06:51.133 --> 00:06:55.307
and unworthy of the same
intellectual respect as their brothers.

00:06:56.522 --> 00:07:00.333
Separately, I become obsessed
with female screams.

00:07:00.665 --> 00:07:01.903
Not in a creepy way.

00:07:01.927 --> 00:07:04.261
I'm talking about, like,
those shrieks and squeals

00:07:04.285 --> 00:07:06.283
that fangirls let out at concerts.

00:07:06.307 --> 00:07:07.617
I want to know why it is

00:07:07.641 --> 00:07:11.037
that some people instinctively flinch
when I merely describe the sound,

00:07:11.061 --> 00:07:13.382
like it's painful just to think about it.

00:07:13.847 --> 00:07:16.585
Then I meet Amy Hume.

00:07:16.609 --> 00:07:18.204
She's a voice coach.

00:07:18.228 --> 00:07:20.916
And she blows my mind.

00:07:21.300 --> 00:07:23.323
Because she tells me
that the female voice

00:07:23.347 --> 00:07:25.798
between the ages of 11 and 13

00:07:25.822 --> 00:07:28.790
is one of the most
interesting things to study.

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:29.972
Why?

00:07:29.996 --> 00:07:32.274
Because there's this research
by Carol Gilligan

00:07:32.298 --> 00:07:33.687
that says that is the age

00:07:33.711 --> 00:07:36.948
when girls begin to perform
and alter their voices.

00:07:36.972 --> 00:07:40.345
For example, adding breath for maturity,

00:07:40.369 --> 00:07:43.139
(Imitating vocal fry)
or adding vocal fry for apathy.

00:07:43.163 --> 00:07:45.234
(Laughter)

00:07:45.258 --> 00:07:48.029
But tell me, according to this research,

00:07:48.053 --> 00:07:51.910
when do you reckon boys begin to perform
and alter their voices?

00:07:51.934 --> 00:07:53.124
Now, I guessed 18,

00:07:53.148 --> 00:07:55.481
because "men mature later," right?

00:07:56.299 --> 00:07:57.672
Wrong.

00:07:57.696 --> 00:07:59.753
The answer was four years old.

00:07:59.777 --> 00:08:03.563
Because that is when boys learn
not to cry or squeal.

00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:06.363
That those are not manly sounds.

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:08.991
And that's when I realized

00:08:09.015 --> 00:08:12.759
that a fangirl's shriek
is therefore like a superpower.

00:08:13.728 --> 00:08:14.990
(Laughter)

00:08:15.014 --> 00:08:18.917
Because it's this fearless
and honest expression

00:08:18.941 --> 00:08:21.310
of pure celebration and joy,

00:08:21.334 --> 00:08:23.155
and it's a sound

00:08:23.179 --> 00:08:25.579
they have not forgotten how to make.

00:08:26.704 --> 00:08:29.855
I actually reckon that fangirls
have a second superpower,

00:08:29.879 --> 00:08:31.656
because they know how to do something

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:34.783
that most of my adult friends
have no idea how to do.

00:08:35.133 --> 00:08:39.442
Fangirls know how to love something
without apology or fear.

00:08:40.364 --> 00:08:42.204
My years of researching fangirls

00:08:42.228 --> 00:08:44.331
culminated in this determination

00:08:44.355 --> 00:08:47.442
to write something that celebrates
and vindicates them.

00:08:47.466 --> 00:08:50.656
So I decided to make
this thriller comedy musical

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:53.934
that sounds like a Beyoncé concert
meets rave meets church.

00:08:54.436 --> 00:08:55.903
I called it "Fangirls,"

00:08:55.927 --> 00:08:58.664
and I designed it like a Trojan horse.

00:08:58.688 --> 00:09:00.934
So it appears to make fun
of these young women,

00:09:00.958 --> 00:09:03.301
only to, like, smuggle them
into your heart.

00:09:03.325 --> 00:09:05.887
(Laughter)

00:09:06.199 --> 00:09:07.350
Thanks.

00:09:07.374 --> 00:09:09.594
(Applause)

00:09:09.618 --> 00:09:11.435
At one point --

00:09:11.459 --> 00:09:12.610
Thanks.

00:09:12.634 --> 00:09:15.085
At one point, a girl sings,

00:09:15.109 --> 00:09:17.006
"Why should I hide my feelings?

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.767
Because they annoy you?

00:09:18.791 --> 00:09:21.497
Or because it isn't what the boys do?"

00:09:22.347 --> 00:09:24.307
And as a former fangirl cynic,

00:09:24.331 --> 00:09:27.084
that is the question
that I want to leave you all with.

00:09:27.744 --> 00:09:29.990
Why should fangirls tone it down?

00:09:30.014 --> 00:09:31.641
Because they're crazy?

00:09:31.665 --> 00:09:34.101
Or because our definition of "reasonable"

00:09:34.125 --> 00:09:37.005
is based on what
it is acceptable for men to do?

00:09:38.129 --> 00:09:41.398
What if we rethink the judgments
we've been conditioned to feel

00:09:41.422 --> 00:09:45.081
when we see young women
screaming their lungs out with excitement?

00:09:45.105 --> 00:09:47.688
What if we decided to rethink
the words we use

00:09:47.712 --> 00:09:49.133
to describe that joy,

00:09:49.157 --> 00:09:53.069
and what if we didn't
allow ourselves to diminish girls

00:09:53.093 --> 00:09:55.969
with words that undermine
their intelligence,

00:09:55.993 --> 00:09:58.731
their interests and their capability?

00:09:58.755 --> 00:10:01.731
Because, according to my research,

00:10:01.755 --> 00:10:05.680
they are capable of building a shrine
to Harry Styles's vomit

00:10:05.704 --> 00:10:08.506
on the side of a freeway within two hours.

00:10:08.530 --> 00:10:09.799
(Laughter)

00:10:09.823 --> 00:10:13.768
That takes some executive skills
in logistics and communication.

00:10:13.792 --> 00:10:15.444
(Laughter)

00:10:15.468 --> 00:10:17.663
If that isn't "capable,"
I don't know what is.

00:10:17.687 --> 00:10:24.437
(Applause)

00:10:24.461 --> 00:10:28.248
I reckon, instead of judging fangirls,
we can learn from them.

00:10:28.590 --> 00:10:29.748
We can all die tomorrow,

00:10:29.772 --> 00:10:32.638
so why not love things
while we're still breathing?

00:10:32.662 --> 00:10:34.233
And with that,

00:10:34.257 --> 00:10:36.950
I'd like to ask you all
to try something with me.

00:10:36.974 --> 00:10:38.783
Can I get you all to stand up?

00:10:38.807 --> 00:10:40.918
Stand up if you can, stand up.

00:10:40.942 --> 00:10:42.981
Alright, so here's what's going to happen.

00:10:43.005 --> 00:10:44.724
I'm going to count to three

00:10:44.748 --> 00:10:45.899
and when I finish,

00:10:45.923 --> 00:10:48.090
I'm going to ask every single one of you

00:10:48.114 --> 00:10:50.979
to let out your very best fangirl scream.

00:10:51.003 --> 00:10:52.161
(Laughter)

00:10:52.185 --> 00:10:53.351
Yeah?

00:10:53.375 --> 00:10:55.585
Here is why I am asking you to do this.

00:10:55.609 --> 00:11:01.204
Because if all five-or-so thousand of you
do this and really commit,

00:11:01.228 --> 00:11:05.093
we all get our first chance
to hear that sound

00:11:05.117 --> 00:11:08.156
and to decide that it is not
a crazy sound.

00:11:08.577 --> 00:11:10.044
It is a hopeful sound.

00:11:10.577 --> 00:11:11.911
So shall we do this?

00:11:12.363 --> 00:11:13.815
I said, shall we do this?

00:11:13.839 --> 00:11:14.990
(Audience: Yes!)

00:11:15.014 --> 00:11:18.524
Alright. OK, I am going to cheat
and I'm not going to go full volume,

00:11:18.548 --> 00:11:20.834
because I'm miked
and we don't want to hear that.

00:11:20.858 --> 00:11:23.197
But it means you all
have to go 110 percent.

00:11:23.221 --> 00:11:25.688
You ready? Take a deep breath with me.

00:11:25.712 --> 00:11:27.461
Think of someone you love, let's go,

00:11:27.485 --> 00:11:30.157
one, two, three.

00:11:30.181 --> 00:11:35.798
(Audience screams)

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:43.670
(Laughter and applause)

00:11:45.482 --> 00:11:49.584
You all just sounded stunning

00:11:49.608 --> 00:11:54.246
and as sane and as intelligent

00:11:54.270 --> 00:11:55.444
and as dignified

00:11:55.468 --> 00:11:57.166
as when you walked in this room.

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:58.190
(Laughter)

00:11:58.214 --> 00:11:59.365
Thank you.

00:11:59.389 --> 00:12:01.757
(Applause)

